// Foundational namespaces. `user` is a leaf — it only appears as the namespace
// of subjects in other tuples.
//
// `platform` has two role tiers: listener (view-only) and admin (view + edit +
// manage). These relation names are the source of truth — PlatformRoleName in
// platform.roles.ts mirrors them, and a tuple whose relation isn't declared here
// is invisible to check(). Adding further relations later is non-breaking:
// existing tuples keep working.

namespace user {
}

namespace platform {
  relation listener: user
  relation admin:    user

  permission view   = listener | admin
  permission edit   = admin
  permission manage = admin
}

// A plugin instance (e.g. `deadair.spotify`). `operator` is view-only on
// purpose: it is the grant you hand someone who should see whether Spotify
// is connected without being able to rewrite its credentials.
namespace plugin {
  relation owner:    user
  relation operator: user

  permission view      = owner | operator
  permission configure = owner
  permission enable    = owner
  permission oauth     = owner
}

// A personal API key. It is modelled as an OBJECT rather than as a subject, because the DSL has no
// walk from one subject to another but it does walk from an object to a parent: `station` links the
// key to `platform:main`, so `station->view` asks whether the key's OWNER may view the station, and
// it asks live. A key therefore shrinks with its owner, the moment a role is taken away, and can
// never be more than the account it acts for, because the intersection is here in the model rather
// than in whichever service remembered to check.
//
// `station` looks redundant with one station per install, and it is not: the DSL cannot name a
// constant object, so without the tuple there is nothing for `->` to walk and nothing to inherit.
// Today it always points at `platform:main`. If several stations ever share one install, it stops
// being constant, and a key is then a key to one of them.
//
// `scoped_view` and `scoped_manage` are the grant chosen at creation, written for the owner. The
// part of the name after `scoped_` IS the scope word the API and the console use
// (`api.key.scopes.ts` derives the vocabulary from these relation names), so a new scope is a new
// relation here and nothing else. `manage` includes `view`, as it does on the platform.
//
// A delegated grant of any other kind (an MCP client authorised through a browser, say) is the same
// shape: an object owned by a user, linked to the station, narrowed by `scoped_*` relations.
namespace apikey {
  relation owner:         user
  relation station:       platform
  relation scoped_view:   user
  relation scoped_manage: user

  permission view   = owner & (scoped_view | scoped_manage) & station->view
  permission manage = owner & scoped_manage & station->manage
  permission revoke = owner
}
